  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 316 PM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nThe large scale pattern features broad ridging across the\nsouthern CONUS, which has flattened in response to a trough \nmoving over Saskatchewan and Manitoba. Surface low pressure and \na cold front are moving over our region currently with scattered\nshowers over northwest MN. There is a narrow corridor of \nelevated instability that could support a few strong storms, \nhowever marginal shear and skinny/elevated CAPE profiles will \nlimit the strength of updrafts. Another mid level trough moves \nacross Canada Monday into Tuesday, and eventually deepens near \nthe Hudson Bay region, resulting in a shift to more northwest \nflow and cooler temperatures (70s for highs). By late next week \nridging is shown by ensembles to begin to build back into the \nNorthern plains though the strongest height rises remain south. \nThis should bring above average temperatures back to the region \nand on the backside of the ridge either a west or southwesterly \nflow aloft. That could support additional progressive waves and \nrebuilding instability towards our region, though there remains \nhigher spread in details. \n\n...Severe Thunderstorm Risk Monday...\n\nThe strongest part of the upper trough remains north of our\nregion, and there is still quite a bit of spread regarding the \ntiming of PVA/forcing along the base of the upper low in Canada.\nThere is at leas a signal for showers and thunderstorms with \n40-60% probabilities for wetting rain (0.1\"+). However, the \npotential for severe thunderstorm impacts will depend on the \ntiming of both the larger scale forcing and surface \nfeatures/mesoscale details which are still carrying lower \npredictability/higher spread with multiple scenarios on \nlocation, timing, coverage of severe coverage. Machine learning \nand AI based systems still highlight a 5%+ potential for severe \nstorms Monday afternoon into Monday night in southeast ND \ntowards parts of west central and northwest MN.",
